#
# `Requires.private: edgefirst-decoder`, and NOT `Requires: edgefirst-tensor`.
# Both halves of that are deliberate.
#
# This file previously read `Requires: edgefirst-tensor`, with a comment
# explaining that "tracking consumes detection lists declared in tensor.h".
# That was true once. `ef_detect_box` has since moved to `edgefirst-decoder-abi`
# and is emitted into `decoder.h`, and this file was never updated to follow --
# so it named a dependency this library does not have and omitted the one it
# does. Nothing caught it because nothing has ever consumed these .pc files:
# the modular C libraries are not yet released, so no compile has ever run
# through this metadata.
#
# WHAT THIS LIBRARY ACTUALLY NEEDS:
#
#   * At COMPILE time, decoder's header. `tracker.h` includes
#     <edgefirst/decoder.h> for `ef_detect_box`, so a consumer needs decoder's
#     include path or the first `#include` fails. (decoder.h in turn includes
#     tensor.h, so tensor's cflags arrive transitively through decoder's own
#     Requires -- they do not need naming here.)
#
#   * At DYNAMIC LINK time, nothing. `libedgefirst_tracker.so` has no
#     DT_NEEDED entry for either sibling: detections cross as a plain C array
#     (`ef_detect_box_list_data`), which is exactly what lets the tracker read
#     a list without linking the library that owns it. That separation is a
#     design point, not an accident, and a plain `Requires:` would undo it by
#     contributing decoder's `Libs:` to every dynamic consumer's link line.
#
# `Requires.private` is the construct that expresses precisely this: its
# Cflags always apply, so the header resolves; its Libs apply only under
# `pkg-config --static`, where a static link genuinely does need decoder's
# symbols present. Correct in both modes rather than a compromise between
# them.

# RELOCATABLE, deliberately -- do NOT hardcode a prefix here.
#
# These libraries ship as tarballs, and a tarball is extracted wherever the
# user wants it. This file previously read `prefix=/usr`, which meant
# pkg-config emitted `-I/usr/include` and `-L/usr/lib` and then ELIDED both as
# system defaults -- so `pkg-config --cflags --libs` returned bare `-l` flags
# with no search paths at all. Extracted anywhere other than over `/usr`, the
# consumer's very first `#include <edgefirst/...>` failed. Nothing caught it
# because these libraries were not released, so no compile had ever run
# through this metadata.
#
# `${pcfiledir}` is the directory holding this .pc file. It sits at
# <prefix>/lib/pkgconfig, so `../..` is the prefix wherever the tarball
# landed. Installing to /usr still works: prefix resolves to /usr and
# pkg-config elides the system paths, which is correct there.
prefix=${pcfiledir}/../..
exec_prefix=${prefix}
libdir=${exec_prefix}/lib
includedir=${prefix}/include

Name: edgefirst-tracker
Description: EdgeFirst multi-object tracking
Version: @VERSION@
Requires.private: edgefirst-decoder
Libs: -L${libdir} -ledgefirst_tracker
Libs.private: -lm -ldl -lpthread
Cflags: -I${includedir}
